    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Sequential vs concurrent",
      "question": "Why can await-ing two coroutines in a row take twice as long, and how do you fix it?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. Two coroutines each wait on the network. Why can awaiting them one after the other take twice as long, and how do you run them concurrently? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Why is <em>await</em>, <em>await</em> slow, and how do you run both at once?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Awaiting one coroutine and then the next runs them one after the other: the first must finish before the second even starts, so the waits add up, to three seconds. To overlap them, schedule both first, with create task or gather, and then wait. Now the total is the longest wait: two seconds.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Add the waits, or <em>overlap</em> them",
            "lang": "python",
            "left": {
              "at": "after",
              "label": "Sequential",
              "title": "≈ 3 s: the sum",
              "tone": "bad",
              "code": "await say_after(1, \"hello\")\nawait say_after(2, \"world\")"
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "overlap",
              "label": "Concurrent",
              "title": "≈ 2 s: the max",
              "tone": "good",
              "code": "await asyncio.gather(\n    say_after(1, \"hello\"),\n    say_after(2, \"world\"))"
            },
            "verdict": {
              "at": "longest",
              "text": "Schedule everything first, then await.",
              "code": "create_task · gather"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Three tools. Gather returns the results in argument order. If one raises, the first exception propagates, but the others keep running, unless you pass return exceptions. Task group, since Python three eleven, is structured concurrency: if one task fails, its siblings are cancelled, and the errors are raised together. And add timeouts with asyncio timeout, or wait for.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Running things <em>together</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "order",
                "label": "gather",
                "title": "Results in order",
                "body": "Matches the argument order.",
                "code": "await gather(a, b)"
              },
              {
                "at": "raises",
                "label": "Errors",
                "title": "First one wins",
                "body": "Others keep running; or return_exceptions=True.",
                "code": "return_exceptions"
              },
              {
                "at": "structured",
                "label": "TaskGroup",
                "title": "Structured",
                "body": "A failure cancels the siblings (3.11+).",
                "code": "async with TaskGroup()"
              },
              {
                "at": "timeouts",
                "label": "Timeouts",
                "title": "Bound the wait",
                "body": "Cancel slow work cleanly.",
                "code": "asyncio.timeout(5)"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
