    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Pitfalls",
      "question": "What are the classic asyncio mistakes?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. What are the classic asyncio mistakes, the ones that silently run nothing, or freeze everything? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "The classic <em>asyncio</em> mistakes?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Four classics. Forgetting await: you get a coroutine object that never runs, and only a warning. Calling blocking code, like time sleep or requests, freezes the whole loop; use the async version, or asyncio to thread. Fire-and-forget tasks need a reference, because the loop only holds them weakly, and they can be garbage collected mid-flight. And CPU-heavy work still blocks the loop: send it to a process pool.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Four <em>classics</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "classics",
                "label": "No await",
                "title": "Never runs",
                "body": "Just a coroutine object and a warning.",
                "code": "never awaited"
              },
              {
                "at": "blocking",
                "label": "Blocking calls",
                "title": "Frozen loop",
                "body": "time.sleep, requests: use async or to_thread.",
                "code": "asyncio.to_thread"
              },
              {
                "at": "reference",
                "label": "Lost tasks",
                "title": "Keep a reference",
                "body": "The loop holds tasks weakly.",
                "code": "tasks.add(t)"
              },
              {
                "at": "still",
                "label": "CPU work",
                "title": "Process pool",
                "body": "Coroutines don't parallelize compute.",
                "code": "run_in_executor"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. Calling an async function only creates a coroutine. Await suspends it and yields to the event loop. Schedule work first, with gather or a task group, so waits overlap. And never block the loop: use async libraries, threads for blocking calls, processes for CPU.",
          "props": {
            "title": "asyncio, <em>in four lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "creates",
                "text": "Calling async def only creates a coroutine.",
                "code": "coroutine object"
              },
              {
                "at": "yields",
                "text": "await suspends and yields to the loop.",
                "code": "one thread"
              },
              {
                "at": "overlap",
                "text": "Schedule first so waits overlap.",
                "code": "gather · TaskGroup"
              },
              {
                "at": "block",
                "text": "Never block the loop.",
                "code": "to_thread · processes"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
